// flow_rate is how much of a cell's fill (0..1000) may transfer to a
// neighbour per tick, in the compressible-volume liquid model -- see
// update.rs's module doc. Higher looks runnier and settles flat faster;
// lower looks viscous. (dispersion is Gas kind's equivalent, not this
// material's -- Liquid kind ignores it.)
//
// 1000 (i.e. no cap below a cell's own full fill) is deliberate, not an
// oversight: `transfer_liquid_horizontal` already bounds each tick's
// actual transfer to half the src/dst difference (see that function's own
// doc for why -- transferring more overshoots and oscillates forever), so
// flow_rate only ever throttles *on top of* that halving. At the original
// 200, flow_rate was the binding constraint for any pool more than a
// handful of cells wide, and a wide pool poured onto an unwalled ledge
// stayed visibly sloped -- sand-pile-shaped, not flat -- for hundreds to
// thousands of frames while flow_rate-limited transfers cascaded outward
// one small hop at a time (reported directly from a live playtest
// screenshot; reproduced with a debug harness pouring water on two
// separate floating ledges and confirmed the same trapezoidal shape).
// Removing flow_rate as a *second*, redundant bottleneck lets the halving
// rule alone govern convergence speed, which converges geometrically
// (each step closes half the remaining gap) rather than linearly.
(
    name: "water",
    display: "Water",
    kind: Liquid,
    density: 1.0,
    flow_rate: 1000,
    // Dead band: below this fill difference two adjacent cells stop trading.
    // 16 is flat but slow; 60 roughly halves settling time (and so how long
    // a pool keeps its chunks awake) for a ~0.5% surface slope that is not
    // visible. Live-tunable with `O` -- sweep it on real water rather than
    // guessing. See `MaterialDef::min_transfer` for the measurements.
    min_transfer: 16,
    // How much a partially-filled cell dims toward black, 0..1. 0.65 is the
    // long-standing look. Lower it if the settled waterline reads as a
    // mottled band rather than a clean edge: only ~1% of a resting body's
    // cells are partial, but they are all at the surface, and their fill
    // ranges widely enough to draw anywhere from ~54% to 100% brightness.
    // Live-tunable with `O`.
    //
    // Restored from 0.0 (2026-08): the zero sat here through the whole world
    // review, was flagged as an open owner question, and the owner's answer
    // was "no idea" -- leftover sweep state, not a choice. Back to the value
    // this comment has always named.
    fill_dimming: 0.65,
    // Standing water dries up into the air above it -- see evaporation.rs
    // for why a puddle goes and a lake does not without anything measuring
    // the size of either. Opt-in per material: lava and oil keep their
    // volume, and the flag is read at the sweep's own dispatch site.
    evaporates: true,
    // The thermal opt-in (the water-cycle milestone). This is the single
    // most expensive line in the file: any finite threshold takes every
    // water cell in the world off fire.rs's thermally-inert fast path, so
    // every *visited* water cell now pays four neighbour temperature reads
    // (the regression README.md's heat_conductivity note records was
    // measured and reverted when conductivity was handed out by default).
    // Re-measured deliberately on examples/ascii.rs's half-water stress
    // scene before shipping -- see the commit that added these lines for
    // the numbers. The conductivity itself is what lets a burning slick or
    // lava actually heat the pool (ignition's is_burning shortcut has no
    // boiling analogue), and what lets meltwater and condensate settle
    // back to ambient so their chunks can sleep.
    heat_conductivity: 0.08,
    boiling_point: 100.0,
    boils_into: "steam",
    // The downward half of the same opt-in, and it costs nothing extra:
    // water is already off the thermally-inert fast path because of the
    // two lines above, so freezing is carried by a threshold compare on a
    // cell the sweep was visiting anyway.
    //
    // Zero exactly, which makes the cold weather.rs writes (a couple of
    // degrees below zero at the mildest, ten below in a hard front) the
    // only thing that can freeze standing water -- ambient is 20 and
    // nothing else in the world is cold. `fire.rs` gates the transition on
    // fill as well, so the partial fringe at a pool's surface stays liquid
    // and only near-full cells become solid; see its `FREEZE_MIN_FILL`.
    cooling_point: 0.0,
    cools_into: "ice",
    // **Four tones, and the fourth exists to fix a weighting bug rather than
    // to add a colour.** `worldgen::passes::ponds` writes shades 0..3 (its
    // `loose_shade` draws against `TONES = 4`), so against a three-entry list
    // `render.rs`'s `palette[shade % len]` folded shade 3 back onto entry 0
    // and that entry drew twice as often as the other two -- a resting pool
    // was 50% one tone and 25% each of the others, where it was meant to be
    // even thirds.
    //
    // The fourth is an **interpolation between the existing entries, not a
    // new hue**: exactly the midpoint of (64, 116, 208) and (72, 126, 218).
    // The set now runs 58 / 64 / 68 / 72 in red, which is the same range the
    // three always spanned with the gap between the two lightest filled in.
    // Water's look is unchanged; only how often each tone is drawn is.
    //
    // Note this also gives the brush four water tones instead of three
    // (`world.rs`'s `paint_stroke` draws its entry from `base_shades`, which
    // is the whole list for any material without a `base_colors` cap). That
    // is the same evenly-weighted set, so painted water matches generated
    // water, which it did not quite do before.
    //
    // `fill_dimming` above is deliberately untouched: it is 0.0, that is a
    // real finding of its own (round 1, finding 1c), and it is an open owner
    // question rather than this task's.
    colors: [
        (64, 116, 208),
        (58, 108, 198),
        (72, 126, 218),
        (68, 121, 213),
    ],
)
